yConfChange says it must be called whenever a configuration change is observed in Ready.CommittedEntries, potentially imposing a stronger ordering than ordinary commands.\",\n          \"Existing test checks that a committed unaccounted configuration change blocks campaigns, but advancing its test log permits campaign; it does not independently exercise delayed host configuration installation.\",\n          \"No legal stale-configuration campaign or safety endpoint has been executed.\"\n        ],\n        \"unknowns\": [\n          \"Does the interface allow delaying the configuration callback until after early Advance, or only delaying other application work?\",\n          \"Can a public Node schedule retain the pending configuration while a subsequent Campaign reaches old tracker membership, with all persistence and batch-order duties met?\"\n        ],\n        \"priority\": 3,\n        \"trigger_rationale\": \"Node.Advance explicitly permits ongoing application while hasUnappliedConfChanges trusts raftLog.applied. RawNode Advance mechanically synthesizes the application acknowledgment. This producer/consumer mismatch needs a concrete caller-duty interpretation; source reading is preferable to assuming an unsafe schedule. The pending-read lead has weaker applicability because ReadIndex explicitly permits loss, and persistence-before-send is independently required by Ready.\"\n      },\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"parent_candidate_id\": null,\n      \"fork_reason\": \"\",\n      \"stop_reason\": \"The core map is usable. Select the concrete applied-frontier/campaign-eligibility premise for contract investigation before constructing any execution requirement.\",\n      \"resume_conditions\": [],\n      \"obligation_id\": null,\n      \"result_claim_ids\": [],\n      \"open_issue_ids\": [],\n      \"executions\": [],\n      \"record\": {\n        \"path\": \"state.json\",\n        \"collection\": \"question_candidates\",\n        \"match\": {\n          